1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,928
A library puts books
on shelves by subject.

2
00:00:02,928 --> 00:00:05,321
A school puts students in classes by age.

3
00:00:05,321 --> 00:00:08,626
And chemists sort millions of
carbon compounds into families.

4
00:00:09,026 --> 00:00:10,181
Three sorting jobs.

5
00:00:10,181 --> 00:00:12,290
Each one uses a few simple questions.

6
00:00:12,740 --> 00:00:14,458
Diya sees it in the kitchen.

7
00:00:14,458 --> 00:00:16,706
She is putting jars back on the shelf.

8
00:00:16,706 --> 00:00:18,714
Sugar, oil, vinegar, and more.

9
00:00:19,064 --> 00:00:19,964
By colour?

10
00:00:19,851 --> 00:00:20,751
By size?

11
00:00:20,548 --> 00:00:23,805
Then she notices vinegar and
lemon juice are both sour.

12
00:00:23,805 --> 00:00:25,379
Same taste, same shelf.

13
00:00:25,879 --> 00:00:30,200
Chemists sort carbon compounds the
same way, by shape and by behaviour.

14
00:00:30,200 --> 00:00:33,670
By the end, you will place
any compound in its family.

15
00:00:34,320 --> 00:00:35,602
Why sort at all?

16
00:00:35,602 --> 00:00:38,194
There are millions of organic compounds.

17
00:00:38,544 --> 00:00:41,139
Learning each one alone is impossible.

18
00:00:41,139 --> 00:00:43,951
But compounds in one family behave alike.

19
00:00:44,301 --> 00:00:46,056
So chemists ask two questions.

20
00:00:46,056 --> 00:00:48,778
First, what does the
carbon skeleton look like?

21
00:00:48,778 --> 00:00:50,147
A chain, or a ring?

22
00:00:50,497 --> 00:00:53,250
Second, which special group
of atoms does it carry?

23
00:00:53,650 --> 00:00:56,168
That special group is
called the functional group.

24
00:00:56,168 --> 00:00:58,008
It decides how the compound reacts.

25
00:00:58,408 --> 00:01:01,035
Skeleton first, functional group next.

26
00:01:01,035 --> 00:01:01,935
Let's sort.

27
00:01:02,582 --> 00:01:06,034
Let's build the family
tree, on our molecule bench.

28
00:01:06,384 --> 00:01:07,408
Question one.

29
00:01:07,408 --> 00:01:08,702
Is there a ring?

30
00:01:08,702 --> 00:01:12,677
If the carbons form an open
chain, the compound is acyclic.

31
00:01:12,677 --> 00:01:15,717
Cyclic means ring, so
acyclic means no ring.

32
00:01:16,117 --> 00:01:20,774
A chain can be straight, like
butane, or branched, like isobutane.

33
00:01:20,774 --> 00:01:22,095
Both are acyclic.

34
00:01:22,095 --> 00:01:24,893
These are also called aliphatic compounds.

35
00:01:25,293 --> 00:01:29,039
If the carbons close into a
ring, the compound is cyclic.

36
00:01:29,039 --> 00:01:30,028
Question two.

37
00:01:30,028 --> 00:01:31,775
Is the ring like benzene?

38
00:01:32,175 --> 00:01:34,465
A ring like cyclohexane is alicyclic.

39
00:01:34,465 --> 00:01:36,718
It behaves much like an open chain.

40
00:01:37,118 --> 00:01:41,388
Benzene and its relatives, like
aniline and naphthalene, are aromatic.

41
00:01:41,388 --> 00:01:42,730
These are benzenoid.

42
00:01:42,730 --> 00:01:47,103
A few special rings without benzene,
like tropolone, are also aromatic.

43
00:01:47,103 --> 00:01:48,689
These are non benzenoid.

44
00:01:49,089 --> 00:01:50,185
Question three.

45
00:01:50,185 --> 00:01:52,152
Is every ring atom a carbon?

46
00:01:52,152 --> 00:01:56,831
If an oxygen, nitrogen or sulphur
sits in the ring, it is heterocyclic.

47
00:01:56,831 --> 00:01:58,671
Tetrahydrofuran is alicyclic.

48
00:01:58,671 --> 00:02:01,470
Furan, thiophene and
pyridine are aromatic.

49
00:02:02,120 --> 00:02:03,742
Now the second question.

50
00:02:03,742 --> 00:02:05,163
The functional group.

51
00:02:05,563 --> 00:02:10,263
It is an atom, or a small group of
atoms, joined to the chain in a fixed

52
00:02:10,263 --> 00:02:11,163
way.

53
00:02:10,755 --> 00:02:13,872
It gives the compound its
main chemical behaviour.

54
00:02:14,272 --> 00:02:17,393
OH is the hydroxyl
group, found in alcohols.

55
00:02:17,393 --> 00:02:19,450
CHO is the aldehyde group.

56
00:02:19,850 --> 00:02:22,379
COOH is the carboxylic acid group.

57
00:02:22,379 --> 00:02:26,460
Vinegar is sour because of acetic
acid, which carries this group.

58
00:02:27,110 --> 00:02:28,010
Now, families.

59
00:02:27,801 --> 00:02:28,701
Link by link.

60
00:02:28,788 --> 00:02:29,688
Link one.

61
00:02:29,583 --> 00:02:34,019
A family of compounds with the same
functional group is called a homologous

62
00:02:34,019 --> 00:02:34,919
series.

63
00:02:35,408 --> 00:02:36,308
Link two.

64
00:02:36,204 --> 00:02:39,764
Each member is one CH two
bigger than the one before.

65
00:02:39,764 --> 00:02:41,733
Methanol, ethanol, propanol.

66
00:02:42,483 --> 00:02:43,383
Link three.

67
00:02:43,354 --> 00:02:45,607
One CH two adds twelve plus two.

68
00:02:45,607 --> 00:02:48,523
So neighbours differ in
mass by fourteen units.

69
00:02:49,273 --> 00:02:50,173
Link four.

70
00:02:50,119 --> 00:02:52,812
One general formula fits the whole family.

71
00:02:52,812 --> 00:02:55,184
Alkanes are C n H two n plus two.

72
00:02:55,934 --> 00:02:56,834
Link five.

73
00:02:56,782 --> 00:03:00,525
Members react alike, because
they share a functional group.

74
00:03:00,525 --> 00:03:05,078
Their physical properties, like boiling
point, change step by step as they

75
00:03:05,078 --> 00:03:05,978
grow.

76
00:03:06,374 --> 00:03:08,741
Now compare two molecules side by side.

77
00:03:08,741 --> 00:03:09,988
Ethane and ethanol.

78
00:03:10,338 --> 00:03:12,729
Both have the same two carbon chain.

79
00:03:12,729 --> 00:03:13,977
Ethane is a gas.

80
00:03:13,977 --> 00:03:15,874
It does not mix with water.

81
00:03:16,274 --> 00:03:19,294
Ethanol has one OH in place of a hydrogen.

82
00:03:19,294 --> 00:03:22,616
It is a liquid, and it
mixes with water easily.

83
00:03:23,016 --> 00:03:24,092
Pause and think.

84
00:03:24,092 --> 00:03:27,784
What made the difference: the
carbon chain, or the OH group?

85
00:03:30,984 --> 00:03:32,156
The OH group!

86
00:03:32,156 --> 00:03:33,781
The chain is the same.

87
00:03:33,781 --> 00:03:36,499
The functional group
changes the behaviour.

88
00:03:36,499 --> 00:03:38,440
That is why we sort by it.

89
00:03:39,090 --> 00:03:40,060
Remember this.

90
00:03:40,060 --> 00:03:41,637
Skeleton tells the shape.

91
00:03:41,637 --> 00:03:43,813
Functional group tells the behaviour.

92
00:03:44,513 --> 00:03:46,246
Now, one worked example.

93
00:03:46,246 --> 00:03:51,076
Find the third member of the alkane
family, its mass, and the next member.

94
00:03:51,376 --> 00:03:52,276
Step one.

95
00:03:52,226 --> 00:03:55,452
The general formula is
C n H two n plus two.

96
00:03:56,202 --> 00:03:57,102
Step two.

97
00:03:57,028 --> 00:03:59,680
The third member has n equal to three.

98
00:03:59,680 --> 00:04:01,193
So C three H eight.

99
00:04:01,193 --> 00:04:02,433
This is propane.

100
00:04:03,183 --> 00:04:04,083
Step three.

101
00:04:03,927 --> 00:04:04,827
Its mass.

102
00:04:04,591 --> 00:04:07,455
Three carbons at twelve,
plus eight hydrogens at one.

103
00:04:07,455 --> 00:04:09,695
Thirty six plus eight, forty four units.

104
00:04:10,445 --> 00:04:11,345
Step four.

105
00:04:11,307 --> 00:04:13,663
The next member adds one CH two.

106
00:04:13,663 --> 00:04:15,346
C four H ten, butane.

107
00:04:15,346 --> 00:04:18,286
Forty four plus fourteen,
fifty eight units.

108
00:04:19,036 --> 00:04:23,849
Both are open chains with no functional
group beyond carbon and hydrogen.

109
00:04:23,849 --> 00:04:26,090
Acyclic, in the alkane family.

110
00:04:26,740 --> 00:04:28,204
Now, the exam trap.

111
00:04:28,554 --> 00:04:31,425
Students think every ring
compound is aromatic.

112
00:04:31,425 --> 00:04:33,565
They put cyclohexane with benzene.

113
00:04:33,965 --> 00:04:34,865
Wrong.

114
00:04:34,558 --> 00:04:36,462
A ring alone is not enough.

115
00:04:36,462 --> 00:04:40,299
Cyclohexane has only single
bonds, and behaves like a chain.

116
00:04:40,299 --> 00:04:41,494
It is alicyclic.

117
00:04:41,494 --> 00:04:45,546
Aromatic means a benzene type
ring, or a special ring like it.

118
00:04:46,196 --> 00:04:47,552
Where is this asked?

119
00:04:47,552 --> 00:04:51,903
Boards and entrance tests ask you to
classify a compound, name its functional

120
00:04:51,903 --> 00:04:53,624
group, or spot homologues.

121
00:04:54,224 --> 00:04:55,394
Exam level now.

122
00:04:55,394 --> 00:04:59,418
Which pair are next door members
of the same homologous series?

123
00:04:59,418 --> 00:05:00,638
Pause and solve.

124
00:05:06,038 --> 00:05:07,454
The answer is A.

125
00:05:07,454 --> 00:05:09,079
Methanol and ethanol.

126
00:05:09,579 --> 00:05:11,501
Both carry the OH group.

127
00:05:11,501 --> 00:05:14,062
They differ by exactly one CH two.

128
00:05:14,562 --> 00:05:16,914
B has no common functional group.

129
00:05:16,914 --> 00:05:19,912
Ethane is an alkane, ethene is an alkene.

130
00:05:20,412 --> 00:05:21,829
C swaps the group.

131
00:05:21,829 --> 00:05:26,368
D is the same family, but two
CH two apart, so not next door.

132
00:05:26,368 --> 00:05:29,255
D traps students who check only the group.

133
00:05:29,855 --> 00:05:31,396
Here is your recap.

134
00:05:31,396 --> 00:05:32,980
Screenshot this card.

135
00:05:36,180 --> 00:05:38,366
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

136
00:05:38,766 --> 00:05:39,666
One.

137
00:05:39,356 --> 00:05:42,132
Is cyclohexane aromatic or alicyclic?

138
00:05:42,932 --> 00:05:43,832
Two.

139
00:05:43,343 --> 00:05:45,615
Name the functional
group in ethanoic acid.

140
00:05:46,415 --> 00:05:47,315
Three.

141
00:05:46,830 --> 00:05:49,325
By how much do two next door
homologues differ in mass?

142
00:05:50,225 --> 00:05:54,205
Next time: why is this molecule
called two methyl pentane?

143
00:05:54,205 --> 00:05:56,594
We find out in IUPAC nomenclature.
